[s0l0!-header][h1]The for Loop[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]for [/b]loop can contain multiple expressions separated by commas in each part. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" ]for (x = 0, y = num; x < y; i++, y--) { 
  statements; 
}[/code]
Also, you can skip the [b]initvalue[/b], [b]condition [/b]and/or [b]increment[/b]. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1659"]int i=0;
int max = 10;
for (; i < max; i++) {
  printf("%d\n", i);
}[/code]
Loops can also be [b]nested[/b]. 
When writing a program this way, there is an outer loop and an inner loop. For each iteration of the outer loop the inner loop repeats its entire cycle.
In the following example, nested for loops are used to output a multiplication table: [code format="c" codeId="1660"]int i, j;
int table = 10;
int max = 12;

for (i = 1; i <= table; i++) {
  for (j = 0; j <= max; j++) {
    printf("%d x %d = %d\n", i, j, i*j);
  }
  printf("\n"); /* blank line between tables */ 
}[/code]
[note]A [b]break [/b]in an inner loop exits that loop and execution continues with the outer loop. 
A [b]continue [/b]statement works similarly in nested loops. [/note]

